.\" Man page for ax25tcpd.conf
.\"
.TH AX25TCPD.CONF 5 "11 Aug 2026" "ax25-apps" "Linux System Administration"
.SH NAME
ax25tcpd.conf \- configuration for ax25tcpd
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ax25tcpd.conf
tells
.BR ax25tcpd (8)
where to find the AGWPE multiplexer it talks to (the back side) and
where it listens for its own clients (the front side).
.PP
A missing file is not an error and leaves the defaults in place:
the back side comes from the defaults of
.BR ax25common.conf (5)
(the unix socket
.IR /var/run/ax25/sockets/ax25netd.sock ,
and no TCP loop port), and the front side is one tcp port on
.BR localhost:8202 ,
with
.BR mtu
256.
.B localhost
covers both the IPv4 and the IPv6 loopback.
.PP
Blank lines and lines beginning with
.B #
are ignored.  A line consists of whitespace separated words.
.SH TARGET
The back side normally comes from the shared loop port configuration
.BR ax25common.conf (5),
the very file the
.BR ax25netd (8)
daemon reads for its listener: one file, one semantics.  The
.B loop socket
and
.B loop tcp
directives there decide how this frontend reaches the daemon, so a
netd on this host needs no
.B target
line at all.  The same file's
.B loop mode
gives the mode of the directory a
.B listen unix
front side creates, so
.B ax25netd
and
.B ax25tcpd
cannot leave different modes on the shared directory depending on
which of them started first.
.PP
For a netd on another host an explicit line overrides it:
.PP
.RS
.B target tcp
.I host tcp-port
.br
.B target socket
.I path
.RE
.PP
.B ax25tcpd
opens a single AGWPE connection to an
.BR ax25netd (8)
loop port.  With
.B target tcp
the connection is a TCP socket to
.IR host : tcp-port ,
with
.B target socket
a unix domain socket at
.IR path .
.SH LISTEN
Each
.B listen
line opens one front side listener and several are allowed.  Without any
.B listen
line the default front side applies (see above).
.PP
.RS
.B listen tcp
.I address port
.br
.B listen unix
.I path [ group all|default|name|gid ]
.RE
.PP
.B listen tcp
binds
.IR address : port .
.I address
is resolved like a host name and every address it resolves to is bound,
so
.B localhost
opens both the IPv4 and the IPv6 loopback; a literal
.B 127.0.0.1
or
.B ::1
restricts it to that one address, while
.B 0.0.0.0
and
.B ::
listen on all IPv4 or all IPv6 interfaces.
.PP
There is one listener per line, not one per mode.  Whether a connection is
.B ascii
or
.B binary
is the client's decision, taken per connection, so a listener serves both
and there is no second port number to configure.  See
.BR MODES
in
.BR ax25tcpd (8).
.PP
What follows from the mode is how a session is framed.  A connected session
.RB ( connect )
is a byte stream in both modes; an
.B ascii
datagram sends every line as one frame, while a
.B binary
datagram treats everything received after the command line as one packet:
it is accumulated and sent as a single AX.25 frame when the connection
closes, split into
.B mtu
sized chunks.
.PP
.B listen unix
listens on a unix domain socket, which is
.B ascii
unless the client says
.BR binary .
With
.B group
the socket is restricted to the given group (a name or a numeric gid);
.B all
makes it world accessible, the default gives it to the daemon's user.
.SH MISCELLANEOUS
.PP
.RS
.B mtu
.I bytes
.RE
.PP
The datagram packet chunk size, 16 to 4096 bytes.  The default is 256.
.PP
.RS
.B default-call
.I callsign
.RE
.PP
The source callsign used when a command line does not give one.  Without
it a connect or datagram command must name its source call explicitly.
.PP
.RS
.B default-port
.I port
.RE
.PP
The port used when a command names no port, so that
.B connect DB0AAA
means something.  It is global, like
.BR default-call ,
and not a property of a listener: a client chooses its mode, but it does
not choose which port a missing one is taken to mean.
.PP
The netd needs a port even to autoroute, so this is what makes the form
without a port more than a synonym for typing the port out.  It has to be a
port the netd knows, with the same spelling as in a command line, so
.B default-port hf
for a high frequency port and
.B default-port loop
for the virtual loopback.  Unset by default: a command without a port is
then refused with a message that names the setting, rather than silently
going out on a port nobody chose.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
	# one listener on 8202, IPv4 and IPv6 loopback
	# back side from ax25common.conf (loop socket/tcp)
	listen	tcp	localhost	8202
	#listen	unix	@@@AX25_LOCALSTATEDIR@@@/ax25tcpd.sock
	#target	tcp	192.0.2.10	8200
	mtu	256
	default-call	DG8HT
	# so that "connect DB0AAA" goes out on the hf port and the
	# netd resolves the digipeater path itself
	default-port	hf
.fi
.SH FILES
.I @@@AX25_SYSCONFDIR@@@/ax25tcpd.conf
.br
.I @@@AX25_SYSCONFDIR@@@/ax25common.conf
(back side unless overridden)
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ax25tcpd (8),
.BR ax25netd (8),
.BR ax25common.conf (5),
.BR ax25netd_agwpe.conf (5)